72:Because the diagnosis pinpoints normalization as the cause, the fix principle is direct. \emph{Raw-Routed Mixture of Adapters} (RR-MoA) routes on the raw, pre-normalization input $\mathbf{X}_\text{raw}$ while keeping the backbone strictly frozen; only the router and five canonical expert heads are trainable. The same principle predicts a \emph{Frozen Paradox}: unfreezing triggers co-adaptation collapse (Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}), so within a fixed MoE architecture frozen should match or beat unfrozen, which we confirm empirically (frozen best on $4/6$, unfrozen-wins bounded at $\leq 13\%$).
230:\textbf{TSFM Adaptation.} For broader context on transformer-based time-series models we refer the reader to the survey by~\citet{wen2023tssurvey}. Outside the TSFM-with-adapter paradigm, a complementary line of work shows that compact task-specific models can match or beat heavyweight pretrained backbones on standard LTSF benchmarks: SRSNet~\citep{wu2025srsnet} learns selective patch-level representations, TimeMixer++~\citep{wang2025timemixerpp} unifies multiple analytical tasks under multi-scale pattern mixing, FITS~\citep{xu2024fits} achieves competitive forecasting with only $10$k parameters via frequency-domain interpolation, TSMixer~\citep{chen2023tsmixer} uses an all-MLP architecture, and~\citet{fu2025selective} trains existing models with timestep-selective losses. The fact that such lightweight raw-input models compete with TSFMs is itself evidence that the per-window raw signal carries most of the predictability we exploit in our diagnostic $R(\mathcal{D})$ (\S\ref{sec:diagnosis}). A parallel adaptation route reprograms pretrained LLMs as forecasters~\citep{jin2024timellm, zhou2023onefitsall, liu2024autotimes}: like RevIN-equipped TSFMs, these inherit per-window normalization upstream of the backbone, so the routing-collapse mechanism we identify applies whenever a downstream MoE adapter is attached. Here we focus on the adapter literature most relevant to RR-MoA. The standard recipe attaches a lightweight module to a frozen TSFM and tunes it on downstream data: LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora}, bottleneck adapters~\citep{houlsby2019adapters}, or a linear probe~\citep{goswami2024moment}. Recent variations explore multi-scale finetuning~\citep{qiao2025msft}, importance-based LoRA selection~\citep{li2025trace}, input-representation adaptation~\citep{benechehab2025adapts}, structured pruning~\citep{zhao2025prune}, in-context tuning~\citep{faw2025icf}, retrieval-augmented adaptation~\citep{ning2025tsrag}, and PEFT alternatives for Chronos~\citep{gupta2024beyondlora}; complementary work computes transferability scores for backbone selection (TEMPLATE,~\citealp{zhang2025template}) or provides a unified evaluation suite for general forecasting models (GIFT-Eval,~\citealp{woo2024gifteval}). These works largely vary the finetuning \textit{strategy} (where to inject parameters, what to prune, what to prompt, which transferability score to use) while leaving the per-input adapter \textit{topology} fixed; AdaPTS~\citep{benechehab2025adapts} proposes several adapter variants and reports the best per dataset/horizon, treating adapter choice as a per-task hyperparameter rather than routing per input window. We instead ask whether the adapter topology should be chosen per input window via routing over heterogeneous experts; the closest precedent in spirit is mixture-of-adapters in the LLM setting (e.g., AdaMix~\citep{wang2022adamix}; AdaMix mixes copies of one adapter type, whereas RR-MoA uses $K$ topologically distinct expert architectures), whereas, in the TSFM literature we surveyed, we did not find prior work that uses a per-window mixture over distinct adapter topologies.
476:\emph{(M2) Within-architecture freeze effect (smaller, $\leq 13\%$, non-unidirectional):} within RR-MoA itself, frozen is best on $4/6$ datasets; light unfreezing wins by up to $13\%$ on the other two; SR-MoA independently replicates the $4/6$ pattern (Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} attributes M2 to gradient co-adaptation: the Router-Detached Gradient Flow ablation (unfreezes backbone but cuts routing-weighted feedback) degrades MSE by $+35$--$100\%$ on three datasets (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}), and disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers full-FT by $54$--$61\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}), pinning the mechanism on normalization rather than optimization. A $90$-config hyperparameter tune (LR $10^{-5}$--$10^{-6}$, $100$ epochs, cosine + warmup, layerwise decay, batch up to $512$) still loses by $51$--$71\%$, consistent with optimization not being the bottleneck within the sweep we ran.}
479:\label{prop:frozen}
480:Consider a $2$-expert MoE with backbone $\mathbf{h}{=}A\mathbf{x}$, router $p{=}\sigma(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{h})$, loss $\ell{=}p\,\ell_1{+}(1{-}p)\,\ell_2$. Under gradient descent on $(A,\mathbf{w})$: \textup{(i)} the dominant expert's loss decreases faster ($\dot{\ell}_k \propto {-}p_k\|\nabla_\mathbf{h}\ell_k\|^2$), and $\dot{p}$ amplifies whichever expert currently has lower loss, creating self-reinforcing collapse toward $p{\in}\{0,1\}$; \textup{(ii)} freezing $A$ removes the co-adaptation feedback through the backbone (full non-collapse additionally requires $(\ell_1{-}\ell_2)$ to flip sign across input regimes, i.e., expert specialization, which is satisfied by the topologically distinct RR-MoA pool). Proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.
