77:\item We formalize the mechanism via a mutual-information decomposition (Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}) and derive a tractable signal-ratio predictor $R(\mathcal{D})$ that anticipates which datasets benefit from raw routing at Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ ($p{<}0.002$), including boundary cases where it correctly does not (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$). The predictor generalizes across three normalization families, five backbones, and a vision modality.
472:In contrast, \textbf{RR-MoA} maintains healthy routing entropy ($1.0$--$1.57$) and wins \textbf{54/54} configurations, with MSE improvements of $26\%$ to $79\%$ over the best fixed adapter. Notably, on Traffic ($R{=}0.14$, where the stripped statistics carry little information), RR-MoA does not improve over fixed adapters, consistent with Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}'s prediction that low $R$ implies little benefit (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}).
511:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Two distinct gains.} \emph{(Q1)} the best fixed adapter on the same RevIN-normalized hidden states still loses to RR-MoA by $26$--$79\%$ (Table~\ref{tab:rrmoa}): residual shape heterogeneity survives normalization and rewards diverse experts; \emph{(Q2)} the signal-ratio $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ measures \emph{dispatch}-signal localization in the stripped statistics, explaining why dense routing also benefits from raw access, and why Traffic ($R{=}0.14$) correctly does not improve. The Pure Raw-MLP MoE ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:raw_mlp_moe}) shows the dataset-conditional choice between ``MoE with raw router'' and ``ensemble with raw input''.}
555:When routing signal lives entirely in the stripped statistics ($(M,\Sigma)\perp\!\!\!\perp S$; Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}), $I(X;E){-}I(S;E){=}H(E)$: RevIN destroys the entire routing distribution. The signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\mathrm{Var}(M)+\mathrm{Var}(\Sigma)]/\overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ is a tractable monotone proxy; the bound is empirically tight (Appendix~\ref{app:mi_tightness}) and learnable $\alpha{<}0.5$ on $30/30$ runs (Appendix~\ref{app:learnable_alpha}). $R(\mathcal{D})$ predicts raw-routing benefit at Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ ($p{<}0.002$, $n{=}9$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}), whereas $H(E\mid S)$ alone does not ($\rho{=}{-}0.12$); Traffic ($R{=}0.14$) is flagged a boundary, Solar ($R{=}0.06$) the one outlier (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}).
565:\textbf{Limitations and outlook.} The diagnosis is specific to per-window normalizers that strip $(\mu,\sigma)$ (RevIN, BatchNorm1d, GroupNorm, InstanceNorm); LayerNorm- and RMSNorm-only backbones need no fix. $R(\mathcal{D})$ is a monotone proxy, not a sharp threshold: it flags Traffic ($R{=}0.14$) correctly, but which intermediate $R$ benefits at which freeze level is open. Evaluation covers forecasting and imputation; classification and anomaly detection remain. The fix needs raw input at routing time, available in adapter-style finetuning but not in purely API-served models; our pool is also fixed at five hand-coded experts (learned pools are open). $R(\mathcal{D})$ itself is portable: any MoE-adapter on a normalized backbone can compute it pre-training, and surfacing this \emph{upstream architectural} cause widens a design space that downstream rescues have so far blocked.\label{page:end_main}
1191:\emph{Tightness condition.} The bound~(ii) is informative when $I(M,\Sigma; E) \gg \varepsilon$. Since $I(M,\Sigma; E)$ increases with the location-scale variance, the signal ratio $R(\mathcal{D}) = [\mathrm{Var}(M) + \mathrm{Var}(\Sigma)] / \overline{\mathrm{Var}(S)}$ is a monotone proxy for the tightness of the bound. This is consistent with the $\rho{=}{-}0.88$ correlation: high-$R$ datasets (Exchange, $R{=}2.17$) have $I(M,\Sigma; E) \gg \varepsilon$, producing large gains ($-68.5\%$); low-$R$ datasets (Traffic, $R{=}0.14$) have $I(M,\Sigma; E) \approx \varepsilon$ (Table~\ref{tab:mi_tightness}, Traffic row: gap $\approx 0.02$ nats), producing no gain ($+2.9\%$).
1221:\emph{Remark on the variance comparison.} Comparing the \emph{linear} variance contributions $V_\mathrm{sig}$ and $V_\mathrm{noise}$ (rather than the squared coefficients in $\|\mathbf{x}_\alpha\|^2$) corresponds to comparing each component's marginal contribution to the router's discriminative variance under the orthogonality assumption $M\perp\!\!\!\perp\Sigma\perp\!\!\!\perp S$. Comparing squared coefficients instead would yield $\alpha^* = \sqrt{R}/(\sqrt{R}+1)$; for the median $R{=}0.67$, the two formulas predict $\alpha^* \approx 0.40$ vs $0.45$, and the dose-response grid (5 points) is too coarse to discriminate. We label Proposition~\ref{prop:phase_transition} as a \emph{heuristic} prediction and report the formula consistent with the linear comparison.
1715:\textbf{Extreme collapse on Exchange and Solar.} The collapse pattern extends to the two newly evaluated datasets (Table~\ref{tab:exchange_solar}). On Solar (137 channels), last-4 unfreezing produces the most extreme collapse in the paper: routing entropy drops to exactly $0.000$ and MSE degrades by $+215$--$243\%$ relative to the best fixed baseline, far worse than the $+0$--$17\%$ degradation observed on the original 6 datasets. On Exchange (8 channels), last-4 AdaMix collapses to $0.000$ entropy but MSE actually improves ($-21\%$), acting as a reasonable single-expert adapter; however, RR-MoA still outperforms it by $2.7\times$ ($1.44$ vs $3.85$).
1837:\textbf{ResNet-18: normalization prevents collapse.} Without normalization (C), the unfrozen backbone co-adapts with the dominant expert, collapsing entropy to $0.184\pm0.260$, consistent with Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen}. Adding InstanceNorm2d (A) or BatchNorm2d (D) \emph{prevents} collapse ($1.48\pm0.02$), the \emph{opposite} of time series. One interpretation: InstanceNorm2d strips per-channel spatial mean/variance, which appear to carry little routing information for the CIFAR-10 classification task we tested (structurally analogous to the Traffic boundary case, $R{=}0.14$).
