utingfree} eliminate the external router; ERMoE~\citep{cheng2025ermoe} replaces softmax+load-balance with eigenbasis-score routing) reaches related conclusions about restructuring the routing decision; in our setting (\S\ref{sec:main_results}), self-gated experts on raw input match RR-MoA, while the same architecture on hidden states collapses. \citet{wang2026myth} argues MoE specialization reflects hidden-state geometry; we extend this upstream by identifying what shapes that geometry on TSFMs.
236:{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Normalization in Time Series.} The instance-level normalization that RevIN extends to time series originated in style transfer~\citep{ulyanov2016instancenorm}; RevIN~\citep{kim2021revin} adapts it to mitigate distribution shift in forecasting; DishTS~\citep{fan2023dishts}, SAN~\citep{liu2023san}, and Non-stationary Transformers~\citep{liu2022nonstationary} partially recover stripped statistics; FAN~\citep{ye2024fan} extends instance normalization beyond $(\mu,\sigma)$ to dominant Fourier components; DDN~\citep{dai2024ddn} dynamically normalizes in both time and frequency domains via wavelet decomposition. Concurrently, \citet{berthelier2026revin} independently show that several RevIN components are redundant or detrimental, and \citet{zou2025ibnorm} argue that variance-centric normalization (BatchNorm, LayerNorm, RMSNorm) is suboptimal for representation learning because two representations with identical mean and variance can encode different amounts of task-relevant information. We identify a specific, mechanistic consequence of this principle that has not been characterized in the MoE literature: when the stripped statistics carry the \emph{routing} signal in a downstream MoE adapter, instance normalization causes the router to collapse to a single expert under unfreezing, a failure mode distinct from the optimization-induced collapse studied in standard MoE rescue literature. Section~\ref{sec:method} formalizes this diagnosis and presents the minimal architectural fix it implies.}
401:RR-MoA's case rests on three claims: that fixed-topology adapters are suboptimal, that the natural MoE remedy collapses on instance-normalized backbones, and that the collapse cannot be recovered by standard rescue mechanisms. We establish each in turn before comparing RR-MoA against the seven-baseline suite (fixed adapters, LoRA, TRACE, independent ensembles, AdaMix, full fine-tuning, DLinear).
485:\textbf{Rescue-baseline sweep.} To rule out that the collapse is merely under-tuned, we sweep five standard MoE rescue families (Switch load-balance~\citep{fedus2022switch}, ST-MoE z-loss~\citep{zoph2022stmoe}, entropy regularization, ReMoE ReLU routing~\citep{wang2025remoe}, and expert-choice routing~\citep{zhou2022expertchoice}) across eleven new rescue configurations plus the load-balance baseline already in Table~\ref{tab:adamix} (twelve configurations total) on 6 datasets $\times$ 2 freeze levels $\times$ 5 seeds $= 720$ run-cells (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}, Appendix~\ref{app:rescue_details}). The best rescue (z-loss $c_z{=}0.01$) reduces MSE by only $10.9\%$, still $2.7{\times}$ worse than RR-MoA. Excessive balancing ($\alpha{=}10.0$) actively hurts MSE ($+67.7\%$). This is consistent with Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}(ii): when routing information is structurally absent from the hidden states, none of the standard rescue mechanisms we tested can compensate.
